\title{Hilbert Class Field Reciprocity and the Unramified $\mathrm{GL}_1$ Correspondence}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 28, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $K$ be a number field with ring of integers $\OK$, and let $H/K$ be a finite Galois extension equipped with an Artin reciprocity isomorphism
\[
A:\Gal(H/K)\xrightarrow{\sim}\Cl(\OK).
\]
We develop the structural consequences of this datum. The Galois group is abelian, and pullback along $A$ gives a canonical group isomorphism between complex characters of the ideal class group and one-dimensional complex representations of $\Gal(H/K)$. This is the finite-order unramified $\mathrm{GL}_1$ correspondence attached to the Hilbert class field. Using the degree formula $[H:K]=h_K$, we further show that degree one is equivalent to class number one, to the principal ideal ring property of $\OK$, and to principality of every nonzero ideal. Finite cyclic algorithms illustrate character transport and clarify the relation to Kronecker--Weber theory, explicit class field theory, and the one-dimensional Langlands correspondence.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Kronecker--Weber theorem describes finite abelian extensions of $\mathbb Q$ through cyclotomic fields. Hilbert's twelfth problem asks for comparably explicit descriptions of abelian extensions of more general number fields. Before explicit generators are supplied, class field theory gives a canonical structural answer at the unramified level: the Hilbert class field converts the ideal class group into a Galois group.

This conversion unifies ideal arithmetic and field symmetry. Although elements in $\OK$ need not factor uniquely, nonzero ideals do, and the class group measures the failure of ideals to be principal. Meanwhile, Frobenius automorphisms in an unramified Galois extension encode the splitting of primes. Artin reciprocity identifies these two groups.

We assume a number field $K$, a finite Galois extension $H/K$, and a group isomorphism
\[
A:\Gal(H/K)\xrightarrow{\sim}\Cl(\OK)
\]
having the role of Hilbert class field reciprocity. The conclusions are consequences of that datum; they do not themselves construct $H$, prove unramifiedness, or provide explicit generators. We prove that the Galois group is abelian, establish the character correspondence, and characterize degree one by principality of ideals.

\section{Algebraic background}

\subsection{Number fields, ideals, and classes}

A \emph{number field} is a finite extension $K/\mathbb Q$. Its ring of integers $\OK$ consists of elements satisfying monic polynomials over $\mathbb Z$. It is a Dedekind domain, so every nonzero ideal factors uniquely into prime ideals.

A nonzero fractional ideal is a nonzero $\OK$-submodule $I\subset K$ such that $dI\subseteq\OK$ for some nonzero $d\in\OK$. Fractional ideals form an abelian group under multiplication. Those of the form $(\alpha)=\alpha\OK$, for $\alpha\in K^\times$, are principal.

\begin{definition}[Ideal class group and class number]
The ideal class group is
\[
\Cl(\OK)=
\{\text{nonzero fractional ideals of }\OK\}/
\{\text{principal fractional ideals}\}.
\]
Its finite cardinality $h_K=|\Cl(\OK)|$ is the class number.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Class-number-one criterion]\label{lem:classone}
The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $h_K=1$;
\item $\Cl(\OK)$ is trivial;
\item every nonzero fractional ideal of $\OK$ is principal;
\item every nonzero integral ideal of $\OK$ is principal;
\item $\OK$ is a principal ideal ring.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A finite group has cardinality one exactly when it is trivial. By the quotient defining the class group, an ideal has trivial class exactly when it is principal. This proves the equivalence of the first three conditions. The third implies the fourth immediately. Conversely, clearing a nonzero denominator turns a fractional ideal into an integral ideal; a generator for the latter, divided by the denominator, generates the former. The fourth condition is precisely the principal-ideal property for nonzero ideals, while the zero ideal is principal.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reciprocity and characters}

For a finite Galois extension let $G=\Gal(H/K)$. The Hilbert class field is the maximal abelian extension unramified at every finite prime. Its Artin map sends unramified prime ideals to Frobenius automorphisms and extends multiplicatively.

\begin{definition}[Hilbert reciprocity datum]
A Hilbert reciprocity datum for $H/K$ is a group isomorphism
\[
A:G\xrightarrow{\sim}\Cl(\OK).
\]
For the Hilbert class field, class field theory also gives
\[
[H:K]=|G|=h_K.
\]
\end{definition}

Write $\mathbb C^\times$ for the multiplicative group of nonzero complex numbers.

\begin{definition}[Characters]
An ideal-class character is a homomorphism $\chi:\Cl(\OK)\to\mathbb C^\times$. A one-dimensional complex Galois representation is a homomorphism $\rho:G\to\mathbb C^\times$.
\end{definition}

Both character sets are abelian groups under pointwise multiplication. Since their source groups are finite, all character values are roots of unity.

\section{Structural results}

\begin{theorem}[Abelian Galois Group Theorem]\label{thm:abelian}
If $A:G\xrightarrow{\sim}\Cl(\OK)$ is a Hilbert reciprocity datum, then $G$ is abelian.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $\sigma,\tau\in G$, commutativity of the class group gives
\[
A(\sigma\tau)=A(\sigma)A(\tau)=A(\tau)A(\sigma)=A(\tau\sigma).
\]
Injectivity of $A$ implies $\sigma\tau=\tau\sigma$.
\end{proof}

Define pullback along reciprocity by $A^*(\chi)=\chi\circ A$, and define transport in the other direction by $(A^{-1})^*(\rho)=\rho\circ A^{-1}$.

\begin{lemma}[Galois-side round trip]
For every $\rho:G\to\mathbb C^\times$,
\[
A^*((A^{-1})^*(\rho))=\rho.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At every $\sigma\in G$, the left side evaluates to $\rho(A^{-1}(A(\sigma)))=\rho(\sigma)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Ideal-side round trip]
For every $\chi:\Cl(\OK)\to\mathbb C^\times$,
\[
(A^{-1})^*(A^*(\chi))=\chi.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At every $c\in\Cl(\OK)$, the left side evaluates to $\chi(A(A^{-1}(c)))=\chi(c)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Multiplicative compatibility]
For ideal-class characters $\chi$ and $\psi$,
\[
A^*(\chi\psi)=A^*(\chi)A^*(\psi).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At $\sigma\in G$, both sides equal $\chi(A(\sigma))\psi(A(\sigma))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unramified $\mathrm{GL}_1$ Correspondence]\label{thm:gl1}
Pullback along Artin reciprocity is an isomorphism of abelian groups
\[
A^*:\Hom(\Cl(\OK),\mathbb C^\times)
\xrightarrow{\sim}
\Hom(G,\mathbb C^\times).
\]
Its inverse is pullback along $A^{-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The two round-trip lemmas show that the pullback maps are mutually inverse. Multiplicative compatibility shows that they preserve the character-group operation.
\end{proof}

The terminology comes from $\mathrm{GL}_1(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C^\times$. Ideal-class characters are the unramified finite-order automorphic parameters in this algebraic model, while the right side consists of one-dimensional Galois representations.

\begin{theorem}[Degree-One Principal-Ideal Criterion]\label{thm:degree}
Suppose the Hilbert class field degree identity $[H:K]=h_K$ holds. Then
\[
[H:K]=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\OK\text{ is a principal ideal ring}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The degree formula gives $[H:K]=1$ if and only if $h_K=1$. Lemma~\ref{lem:classone} identifies the latter condition with the principal ideal ring property.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Principality implies degree one]
If every nonzero ideal of $\OK$ is principal, then $[H:K]=1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis makes $\OK$ a principal ideal ring, so Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Degree one implies principality]
If $[H:K]=1$, then every nonzero ideal of $\OK$ is principal.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:degree} makes $\OK$ a principal ideal ring.
\end{proof}

Thus the triviality of the extension, triviality of the class group, and principality of every nonzero ideal are equivalent.

\section{Finite models and algorithms}

Suppose $\Cl(\OK)\cong C_n=\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z$, generated by $g$. Every complex character is indexed by $k\in\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$:
\[
\chi_k(g^j)=\exp\left(\frac{2\pi i k j}{n}\right).
\]
These exhaust the character group and satisfy $\chi_k\chi_\ell=\chi_{k+\ell\bmod n}$. If $A(\sigma_j)=g^j$, then the transported representation has exactly the same values.

\begin{algorithm}[Cyclic character table]
Given $n>0$, create an $n\times n$ matrix and put
\[
T_{k,j}=\exp(2\pi i k j/n)
\]
in row $k$ and column $j$. Return $T$. The method uses $O(n^2)$ complex arithmetic operations and $O(n^2)$ output storage. Character orthogonality gives the check
\[
\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\chi_k(g^j)\overline{\chi_\ell(g^j)}
=\begin{cases}n,&k=\ell,\\0,&k\ne\ell.\end{cases}
\]
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Transport through a finite Artin table]
Represent $A$ by a permutation $p$, with Galois position $j$ mapped to ideal-class position $p(j)$. For a character vector $v$, set $w_j=v_{p(j)}$. Build $p^{-1}$ in $O(n)$ time; transport and inverse transport each take $O(n)$ time. Applying both must restore $v$.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Degree and principality diagnostic]
Given a class number $h_K$ known to equal $[H:K]$, report extension degree $h_K$, report that $\OK$ is a principal ideal ring exactly when $h_K=1$, and report $h_K$ one-dimensional characters. The decision is constant-time once $h_K$ is supplied; computing $h_K$ from a defining polynomial is a separate problem.
\end{algorithm}

For a general decomposition $\Cl(\OK)\cong C_{n_1}\times\cdots\times C_{n_r}$, write elements as $j=(j_1,\ldots,j_r)$ and characters as $k=(k_1,\ldots,k_r)$. Then
\[
\chi_k(j)=\exp\left(2\pi i\sum_{a=1}^r\frac{k_a j_a}{n_a}\right).
\]
There are $\prod_a n_a=h_K$ elements and characters. Direct construction takes $O(h_K^2r)$ arithmetic operations; tensor products of cyclic Fourier matrices expose faster product structure.

\section{Examples}

For $K=\mathbb Q(i)$, the ring $\mathbb Z[i]$ is Euclidean and hence principal. Thus $h_K=1$, the Hilbert class field has degree one, and both character groups consist only of the trivial character.

For $K=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-5})$, the class number is $2$. The Hilbert class field has degree $2$, and the cyclic character table is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
The nontrivial ideal-class character sends the nonidentity class to $-1$; reciprocity transports it to the Galois representation sending the nonidentity automorphism to $-1$.

For a cyclic class group of order $3$, put $\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}$. The table
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
1&1&1\\
1&\omega&\omega^2\\
1&\omega^2&\omega
\end{pmatrix}
\]
serves simultaneously as the ideal-class and Galois character table after columns are identified by $A$.

For a finite prime $\mathfrak p$ unramified in $H$, reciprocity relates its Frobenius element and ideal class, up to the chosen Frobenius convention. Hence
\[
\rho_\chi(\operatorname{Frob}_{\mathfrak p})=\chi([\mathfrak p]).
\]
A completely split prime has trivial Frobenius, so every transported character takes value $1$ there. Conversely, finite abelian characters separate points; if every character has value $1$ on a Frobenius element, that element is trivial.

\section{Kronecker--Weber, Langlands, and scope}

Kronecker--Weber supplies explicit roots of unity for abelian extensions of $\mathbb Q$. For a general number field, the Hilbert class field is the first canonical unramified abelian layer. Reciprocity determines its Galois group abstractly from ideals, but this is not yet a universal explicit-generation theorem. Hilbert's twelfth problem asks for analytic generators analogous to roots of unity.

Theorem~\ref{thm:gl1} is the finite-order unramified $\mathrm{GL}_1$ correspondence. A fuller formulation uses the id\`ele class group and matches its continuous characters with one-dimensional characters of the abelianized absolute Galois group. Ideal-class characters are those characters factoring through the unramified class-group quotient. In higher rank, the Langlands program compares higher-dimensional Galois representations with automorphic representations of $\mathrm{GL}_n$. The present setting displays the common pattern without higher-rank analytic and nonabelian complications.

The structural assumptions must be kept visible. The reciprocity isomorphism is an input. Existence and maximal unramifiedness of $H$, principalization of ideals in $H$, and explicit defining equations require additional theorems. Likewise, ideal-class characters describe only the unramified finite-order part of global $\mathrm{GL}_1$ reciprocity.

\section{Future directions}

First, one should construct for every number field $K$ a finite Galois extension $H/K$, unramified at all finite primes, together with Artin reciprocity $\Gal(H/K)\cong\Cl(\OK)$. Second, the principal ideal theorem should be established: extension to $H$ makes every fractional ideal of $\OK$ principal, equivalently the map $\Cl(\OK)\to\Cl(\mathcal O_H)$ is zero. Third, an id\`elic realization should identify the kernel of the quotient from the id\`ele class group to $\Cl(\OK)$ and characterize the corresponding characters. Fourth, general reciprocity over $\mathbb Q$ should be compared explicitly with the cyclotomic rule $\zeta\mapsto\zeta^p$. Finally, for imaginary quadratic fields of small class number, singular moduli should be used to construct explicit Hilbert class field generators and establish their degree and unramifiedness.

\section{Conclusion}

The reciprocity mirror
\[
\Gal(H/K)\cong\Cl(\OK)
\]
transfers commutativity from ideals to automorphisms, transports every ideal-class character to a unique one-dimensional Galois representation, and, with the degree formula, equates the triviality of the Hilbert class field with principality of all ideals. The class group measures an obstruction, the Hilbert class field realizes it as symmetry, and character duality records that symmetry as complex phases. This is a precise entry point into explicit class field theory and the Langlands program.

\end{document}

